  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests whether sparse autoencoders recover interpretable features in LLM activations, in the new setting of medical-domain training and comparison with broad-domain GemmaScope SAEs.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports results for reconstruction, loss recovery, residual linear structure, and feature interpretability. Figure 4 compares automated detection and fuzzing scores, but the plotted values are not given numerically in the text. Background statements attributed to other studies are not recorded as results.",
    "test_reason": "It trains SAEs on Gemma-2-2b/9b layer-20 activations in a medical domain and reports reconstruction, loss recovered, dark-matter residuals and feature interpretability against GemmaScope. These bear on V4 (whether SAEs recover sparse interpretable features) and on V5's completeness and canonical-unit claims, since the dictionary changes with training distribution and leaves linear residual. Most rows compare the authors' method with GemmaScope, so they bear on the claim mainly through absolute levels and through the domain dependence they reveal.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also claim that reasonably sized domain-specific SAEs can 'truly learn all required features' for a domain. They argue the field should question foundation-model scaling of general-purpose SAEs. They assert steering benefits that the paper never reports.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Trains JumpReLU SAEs on medical QA data and compares them with broad-domain GemmaScope SAEs; the paper does not reproduce the original toy-model superposition experiments.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported comparison varies training domain, model, SAE width, and L0 sparsity; the paper says the variance-explained advantage holds across tested L0 values.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "Numerical results for several comparisons are not provided in the prose and are plotted without exact values. Interpretability evaluation is restricted to a maximum of 1 000 latent features; the paper reports selected feature examples and does not test stability across training runs or canonicality.",
    "rows_left_out": "Figure 4's automated detection and fuzzing comparisons are not separately included because the figure gives no exact scores and the text does not provide specific numerical outcomes for each model and metric.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "All: no toy models, geometry, or sparsity transition."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "All: no neuron-level polysemanticity or capacity analysis of the model itself. 'Capacity' here refers to the SAE's latent budget."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "All."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Whether features are spread across many neurons. Representative (non-selected) interpretability scores are given only in Figure 4, without numbers."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Stability across seeds. Canonicality beyond the Appendix F cross-SAE matching, which is not quantified in the text."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "All."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "Aggregate detection and fuzzing F1 scores for the domain SAE vs GemmaScope across up to 1,000 latents. This is the only non-selected interpretability evidence.",
        "locator": "Figure 4; Appendix D",
        "versions": "V4"
      },
      {
        "what": "Hungarian matching of GemmaScope features to domain-SAE features (decoder/encoder cosine) and max-cosine distributions. This bears on whether dictionaries share canonical features across training distributions. No numbers are given in the text.",
        "locator": "Appendix F; Figures 8–9",
        "versions": "V5"
      },
      {
        "what": "Steering evaluations are claimed in the Discussion but no results appear in the paper.",
        "locator": "Section 6",
        "versions": "none"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "The study is mainly a method comparison (its SAE vs GemmaScope), so its V4 support is generic and relies on absolute reconstruction levels plus cherry-picked examples. The V5 bearing is that SAE features depend on training data and that linear dark matter persists. The authors' claim of near-completeness is not supported by error-vector R² of about 0.56–0.73. Figure values were read from colour-bar ranges and are approximate. Preprint; no uncertainty reported."
  },
